# xdg-dbus-proxy -- the filtering proxy flatpak puts between a sandboxed
# application and the session bus.
#
# Without it a sandboxed app talking to D-Bus talks to the WHOLE bus, which
# defeats most of the point of the sandbox: the session bus is where the
# screen locker, the keyring and every other service live. flatpak runs this
# proxy per-app and applies the --talk-name and --own-name policy through it.
#
# PACKAGED SEPARATELY RATHER THAN VENDORED, for the same reason as bubblewrap:
# flatpak ships it as a meson subproject (-Dsystem_dbus_proxy=) and would
# otherwise build an invisible copy. This one enforces the D-Bus half of the
# sandbox exactly as bwrap enforces the namespace half, and the same argument
# applies -- a policy enforcement point that is not in the dependency graph
# cannot be audited or updated.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-dbus-proxy/releases/download/0.1.6/xdg-dbus-proxy-0.1.6.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=131bf59fce7c7ee7ecbc5d9106d6750f4f597bfe609966573240f7e4952973a1
SRC_DIR=xdg-dbus-proxy-0.1.6

# -Dtests=false. UPSTREAM DEFAULT IS TRUE. Build-time only, and the tests want a
# session bus to talk to.
#
# -Dman=disabled. At 'auto' it would try; the man pages need tooling that is not
# packaged.
#
# NOT SET, AND WORTH SAYING SO: -Dinstalled_tests. It ALREADY DEFAULTS TO FALSE
# here, which is the opposite of json-glib, where the same option defaults TRUE
# and would have installed test binaries onto every system. Same option name,
# same upstream family, opposite defaults -- so the value is recorded rather
# than the flag passed, because "we checked and it is already off" and "nobody
# looked" are indistinguishable in a recipe that says nothing.
MESON_ARGS="-Dtests=false -Dman=disabled"
